  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nLow clouds continue to clear over southeast Montana and portions\nof northern Wyoming this afternoon. Ridging will generally take\nover the pattern this week, bringing warmer and mostly dry\nconditions.\n\nHiRes models show patchy fog developing along and north of I-90\nfrom Billings to Livingston tonight. Coverage and density are \nexpected to be less than this morning though reductions to\nvisibility will still impact travel. This will continue to be \nmonitored overnight.\n\nModels and ensembles continue to depict a shortwave moving \nthrough the region late Tuesday through early Wednesday. This \nlooks to bring the best chance of precipitation to much of \nsouthern Montana and northern Wyoming this week. Disagreement on \nthe extent of precipitation remains amongst deterministic models, \nthough the latest forecast has a low to moderate chance across the\narea. The best chance exists in the Absaroka/Beartooth, Pryor, \nand Bighorn mountains.\n\nWhile soundings do show CAPE values between 1000 and 2000 J/kg  \nTuesday afternoon/ evening, the lack of sufficient forcing and\nmostly weak shear will likely keep the chance for storms very \nlow. Overnight though, a few thunderstorms could develop as the\nshortwave moves through. Though surface inversions will develop,\nelevated instability of a several hundred J/kg of CAPE will \nlinger mostly in far southeast Montana. The main concerns with any\nstorms are gusty winds, heavy rainfall, and hail. The risk for \nstronger to potentially severe thunderstorms remains low at this \ntime.\n\nThe rest of Wednesday through Friday, the forecast has a very low\nchance (less than 15 percent) for precipitation across the area.\nWinds look to increase over the foothills west of Billings on\nFriday, ahead of a deepening trough over the Pacific Northwest. \nUncertainty remains high with this system over the weekend. The \nlatest ensemble tracks take it through northwest Montana and into \nCanada, leaving southeast MT and northern WY on the drier side of \nthings. Continue to monitor the forecast as the weekend\napproaches.\n\nHigh temperatures are forecast in the 70s to 80 degrees Tuesday\nand Wednesday, and upper 70s to mid 80s Thursday and Friday.\n\nMatos",
